<% content_for :title, "CBT & Bipolar | Bipolar Sober" %>

<main class="cbt-bipolar-page">

  <!-- HERO -->
  <section class="cbt-bipolar-hero">
    <div class="cbt-bipolar-hero-content">

      <p class="cbt-bipolar-eyebrow">
        BIPOLAR SOBER / BIPOLAR / PERSPECTIVES / CBT
      </p>

      <h1>
        <span>BIPOLAR</span>
        <span>THROUGH A</span>
        <span>CBT LENS.</span>
      </h1>

      <p class="cbt-bipolar-intro">
        YOUR BRAIN SAID IT.<br>
        THAT DOESN'T MAKE IT FUCKING TRUE.
      </p>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 01 / THOUGHTS -->
  <section class="cbt-section cbt-pink">

    <div class="cbt-section-heading">
      <p class="cbt-number">
        01 / THOUGHTS
      </p>

      <h2>
        THOUGHT<br>
        ≠ FACT.
      </h2>
    </div>

    <div class="cbt-copy">

      <p class="cbt-lead">
        CBT — Cognitive Behavioral Therapy — looks at the relationship
        between thoughts, emotions, and behavior.
      </p>

      <p>
        Something happens. Your brain interprets it. That interpretation
        affects how you feel. Those feelings can influence what you do next.
      </p>

      <div class="cbt-chain">
        <span>SITUATION</span>
        <b>→</b>
        <span>THOUGHT</span>
        <b>→</b>
        <span>FEELING</span>
        <b>→</b>
        <span>BEHAVIOR</span>
      </div>

      <p>
        The important part is that the thought in the middle isn't
        automatically an objective description of reality.
      </p>

      <blockquote>
        MY BRAIN PRODUCED A THOUGHT.<br>
        NOW LET'S SEE IF THE DAMN THING HOLDS UP.
      </blockquote>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 02 / AUTOMATIC THOUGHTS -->
  <section class="cbt-section cbt-yellow">

    <div class="cbt-section-heading">
      <p class="cbt-number">
        02 / AUTOMATIC THOUGHTS
      </p>

      <h2>
        CATCH THE<br>
        FIRST STORY.
      </h2>
    </div>

    <div class="cbt-copy">

      <p class="cbt-lead">
        Automatic thoughts show up fast — often before we even realize
        we've interpreted anything.
      </p>

      <p>
        Someone doesn't text back.
      </p>

      <div class="cbt-example">
        <p>THE EVENT:</p>
        <h3>THEY DIDN'T TEXT BACK.</h3>

        <p>THE AUTOMATIC THOUGHT:</p>
        <h3>THEY'RE FUCKING MAD AT ME.</h3>

        <p>THE FACT:</p>
        <h3>THEY DIDN'T TEXT BACK.</h3>
      </div>

      <p>
        Maybe they're mad. Maybe they're working. Maybe they're asleep.
        Maybe their phone died. Maybe they're staring directly at the
        message and being an asshole.
      </p>

      <p>
        The point isn't that your interpretation is definitely wrong.
        The point is that you don't know yet.
      </p>

      <blockquote>
        SEPARATE WHAT HAPPENED FROM THE STORY YOUR BRAIN ADDED.
      </blockquote>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 03 / DISTORTIONS -->
  <section class="cbt-section cbt-black">

    <div class="cbt-section-heading">
      <p class="cbt-number">
        03 / COGNITIVE DISTORTIONS
      </p>

      <h2>
        YOUR BRAIN<br>
        HAS SOME<br>
        FAVORITES.
      </h2>
    </div>

    <div class="cbt-copy">

      <p class="cbt-lead">
        Cognitive distortions are common patterns of thinking that can
        twist how we interpret ourselves, other people, and situations.
      </p>

      <div class="cbt-distortions">

        <div>
          <span>ALL-OR-NOTHING</span>
          <p>
            If it isn't perfect, it's a complete failure.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <span>CATASTROPHIZING</span>
          <p>
            Something went wrong, therefore everything is about to explode.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <span>MIND READING</span>
          <p>
            Deciding you know what somebody else thinks without evidence.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <span>FORTUNE TELLING</span>
          <p>
            Treating your prediction of the future like a confirmed fact.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <span>EMOTIONAL REASONING</span>
          <p>
            I feel it, therefore it must be true.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <span>OVERGENERALIZING</span>
          <p>
            One bad thing becomes "this always happens."
          </p>
        </div>

      </div>

      <p>
        Everybody does this shit. The goal isn't to never have a distorted
        thought again.
      </p>

      <p>
        The goal is getting better at recognizing one before you build
        your entire afternoon around it.
      </p>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 04 / BIPOLAR -->
  <section class="cbt-section cbt-purple">

    <div class="cbt-section-heading">
      <p class="cbt-number">
        04 / BIPOLAR
      </p>

      <h2>
        MOOD CAN<br>
        CHANGE THE<br>
        STORY.
      </h2>
    </div>

    <div class="cbt-copy">

      <p class="cbt-lead">
        Mood and cognition affect each other.
      </p>

      <p>
        During depression, thoughts may become more hopeless,
        self-critical, or negative.
      </p>

      <p>
        During elevated states, confidence, optimism, urgency,
        and expectations about what is possible may increase.
      </p>

      <p>
        CBT doesn't mean arguing yourself out of a mood episode.
        Bipolar disorder isn't simply a collection of incorrect thoughts.
      </p>

      <blockquote>
        THE GOAL ISN'T TO THINK YOURSELF OUT OF BIPOLAR.
      </blockquote>

      <p>
        Instead, CBT skills can help you examine interpretations,
        recognize patterns, notice changes, and make decisions with
        more information than the feeling of the moment.
      </p>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 05 / EVIDENCE -->
  <section class="cbt-section cbt-pink">

    <div class="cbt-section-heading">
      <p class="cbt-number">
        05 / EVIDENCE
      </p>

      <h2>
        SHOW ME<br>
        THE FUCKING<br>
        RECEIPTS.
      </h2>
    </div>

    <div class="cbt-copy">

      <p class="cbt-lead">
        Instead of asking whether a thought feels true,
        ask what evidence actually supports it.
      </p>

      <div class="cbt-evidence">

        <p class="cbt-evidence-thought">
          "EVERYBODY THINKS I'M A FAILURE."
        </p>

        <div>
          <span>EVIDENCE FOR IT</span>
          <p>
            What facts actually support this thought?
          </p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <span>EVIDENCE AGAINST IT</span>
          <p>
            What facts don't fit the thought?
          </p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <span>WHAT DON'T I KNOW?</span>
          <p>
            Where am I filling in missing information myself?
          </p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <span>ANOTHER EXPLANATION?</span>
          <p>
            What else could reasonably explain the situation?
          </p>
        </div>

      </div>

      <blockquote>
        FEELINGS ARE INFORMATION.<br>
        THEY ARE NOT COURT VERDICTS.
      </blockquote>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 06 / REFRAME -->
  <section class="cbt-section cbt-yellow">

    <div class="cbt-section-heading">
      <p class="cbt-number">
        06 / REFRAMING
      </p>

      <h2>
        NOT FAKE<br>
        POSITIVE<br>
        BULLSHIT.
      </h2>
    </div>

    <div class="cbt-copy">

      <p class="cbt-lead">
        Reframing doesn't mean replacing every negative thought with
        "everything is wonderful."
      </p>

      <p>
        That's not CBT. That's just lying to yourself with better branding.
      </p>

      <div class="cbt-reframe">

        <div>
          <span>TOO EXTREME</span>
          <p>
            "I ruined everything."
          </p>
        </div>

        <b>↓</b>

        <div>
          <span>MORE ACCURATE</span>
          <p>
            "I fucked up something important. I can figure out what
            can be repaired and what I need to do differently."
          </p>
        </div>

      </div>

      <p>
        A useful reframe isn't necessarily positive.
      </p>

      <p>
        It's <strong>more accurate, more complete, and more useful.</strong>
      </p>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 07 / BEHAVIOR -->
  <section class="cbt-section cbt-purple">

    <div class="cbt-section-heading">
      <p class="cbt-number">
        07 / BEHAVIOR
      </p>

      <h2>
        DO<br>
        SOMETHING<br>
        DIFFERENT.
      </h2>
    </div>

    <div class="cbt-copy">

      <p class="cbt-lead">
        CBT isn't just sitting around interrogating your thoughts.
        The B stands for behavioral for a reason.
      </p>

      <p>
        Behavior can influence mood, beliefs, routines, confidence,
        and the evidence your brain has available.
      </p>

      <p>
        Depression might say:
        <strong>"There's no point getting out of bed."</strong>
      </p>

      <p>
        You don't necessarily have to wait until you feel motivated.
        Sometimes the experiment is getting up, showering, eating,
        or completing one small task and seeing what happens next.
      </p>

      <blockquote>
        YOU DON'T ALWAYS HAVE TO FEEL DIFFERENT
        BEFORE YOU DO SOMETHING DIFFERENT.
      </blockquote>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 08 / PRACTICE -->
  <section class="cbt-section cbt-black">

    <div class="cbt-section-heading">
      <p class="cbt-number">
        08 / PUT IT INTO PRACTICE
      </p>

      <h2>
        CHECK YOUR<br>
        FUCKING<br>
        THINKING.
      </h2>
    </div>

    <div class="cbt-copy">

      <p class="cbt-lead">
        When something hits hard, slow the mental chain down.
      </p>

      <div class="cbt-practice-list">

        <div>
          <span>01</span>
          <p>
            <strong>WHAT HAPPENED?</strong><br>
            Describe the situation without interpretation.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <span>02</span>
          <p>
            <strong>WHAT DID I THINK?</strong><br>
            Catch the automatic story.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <span>03</span>
          <p>
            <strong>WHAT DID I FEEL?</strong><br>
            Name the emotion instead of letting it run the meeting.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <span>04</span>
          <p>
            <strong>WHAT'S THE EVIDENCE?</strong><br>
            Look for facts both for and against your interpretation.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <span>05</span>
          <p>
            <strong>WHAT AM I MISSING?</strong><br>
            Look for distortions, assumptions, and other explanations.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <span>06</span>
          <p>
            <strong>WHAT'S MORE ACCURATE?</strong><br>
            Replace the first story with one that fits all the evidence.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <span>07</span>
          <p>
            <strong>WHAT DO I DO NEXT?</strong><br>
            Choose behavior based on the fuller picture.
          </p>
        </div>

      </div>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- ENDING -->
  <section class="cbt-ending">

    <div>

      <p class="cbt-ending-small">
        YOUR BRAIN HAS OPINIONS.
      </p>

      <h2>
        CHECK THE<br>
        <span>RECEIPTS.</span>
      </h2>

    </div>

    <div class="cbt-ending-copy">

      <p>
        Thoughts matter.
      </p>

      <p>
        Feelings matter.
      </p>

      <p>
        Neither automatically tells the entire story.
      </p>

      <p>
        CBT gives you a way to slow things down, examine the evidence,
        recognize patterns, and decide whether the first story your
        brain handed you deserves to become your next action.
      </p>

      <%= link_to "← BACK TO PERSPECTIVES",
          bipolar_perspectives_path,
          class: "cbt-back-button" %>

    </div>

  </section>

</main>